## SECTION 4 — SERP ANALYSIS
**Competitor gaps (2+ source validated):**
- Almost no top result addresses the **dermatologist-confirmed health tradeoff** — that dip is *not* automatically safer or healthier despite lasting longer (confirmed: Cleveland Clinic + HuffPost/derm sourcing). Most competitors imply "longer-lasting = better" without this nuance.
- Builder gel vs. soft soak-off gel distinction is rarely made clear — many searchers don't realize "gel" can mean two very different durability tiers (2–3wk soft gel vs 3–4wk builder gel, nearly matching dip). Confirmed: Erica's ATA, Quora threads.
- Cost-over-time math (frequency of visits × price) is mentioned but rarely calculated concretely for the reader.
- "Dip flu" / cyanoacrylate vapor sensitivity reaction is a real but under-covered angle (found once, single-source — flagged, use cautiously, don't overstate).

**Missing semantic territory:** builder gel vs soft gel distinction, real cost-per-month math, nail-art flexibility angle, "which chips first" reader concern, sensitive/thin nail guidance.

**Our strategic approach:** Most competitors give a flat "dip lasts longer" verdict and stop there. Our angle: confirm dip *does* generally last longer (3–5 weeks vs. gel's 2–3 weeks) but immediately complicate the "so dip wins" assumption with the health tradeoff and the builder-gel exception — giving the reader a genuinely useful decision framework instead of a hollow durability score.

